(5528573, 797116, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>I''d say you did better than "here goes nothing!"  I, for one, thought the phrasing was very good for this challenge - has an element of humor and matches your photograph - I could see this as an actual ad for the zoo.  I''m also delighted that you took your mom to the zoo - challenges have a way of making us do things we probably wouldn''t otherwise, don''t they?  And finally, congrats on the new toys!  You did well with that lens, particularly with an extender - requires very fast shutter and a steady hand!  You had good crispness in the focus and it was smart of you to wait for the cloud, as the exposure looks good.  AND you got a great look from your subject.  <br/> <br/>The high ISO isn''t all that readily apparent, but does show up a bit in the background.  One option would be to selectively apply noise reduction on the background and see if that improves it a bit.  Since it''s advanced editing, you can create a duplicate layer, apply noise reduction, then erase it off any parts you don''t like the effect on.<br/> <br/>And congrats on a new 5th place entry on your profile page top row!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! I''d say you did better than "here goes nothing!"  I, for one, thought the phrasing was very good for this challenge - has an element of humor and matches your photograph - I could see this as an actual ad for the zoo.  I''m also delighted that you took your mom to the zoo - challenges have a way of making us do things we probably wouldn''t otherwise, don''t they?  And finally, congrats on the new toys!  You did well with that lens, particularly with an extender - requires very fast shutter and a steady hand!  You had good crispness in the focus and it was smart of you to wait for the cloud, as the exposure looks good.  AND you got a great look from your subject.   The high ISO isn''t all that readily apparent, but does show up a bit in the background.  One option would be to selectively apply noise reduction on the background and see if that improves it a bit.  Since it''s advanced editing, you can create a duplicate layer, apply noise reduction, then erase it off any parts you don''t like the effect on. And congrats on a new 5th place entry on your profile page top row!', '2009-06-15 02:46:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(5528579, 796993, 73998, '<td valign="top">Congrats, Enzo!  Your shot was my highest rated of the challenge, so it''s good to see you on the front page too!</td>', 'Congrats, Enzo!  Your shot was my highest rated of the challenge, so it''s good to see you on the front page too!', '2009-06-15 02:58:40.000000', NULL, 0),
